outputs.exact_statement:
Let $\Sigma\subset\mathbb R^3$ be a compact smooth positively curved surface, possibly with smooth boundary. For every $3<p<\infty$, there is a finite constant $C_{\Sigma,p}$ such that $$\label{eq:main}
 \|E_\Sigma f\|_{L^p(\mathbb R^3)}
 \le C_{\Sigma,p}\|f\|_{L^p(\Sigma)}$$ for every complex-valued $f\in L^p(\Sigma)$.

For every $3<p<\infty$ and $s>2-6/p$, the free Schrödinger propagator, initially defined on Schwartz functions, extends boundedly from $W^{s,p}(\mathbb R^2)$ to $L^p(\mathbb R^2\times[0,1])$: $$\label{eq:schrodinger-smoothing}
 \|e^{it\Delta}f\|_{L^p(\mathbb R^2\times[0,1])}
 \le C_{p,s}\|f\|_{W^{s,p}(\mathbb R^2)}.$$

*For a fixed phase as in Equation <a href="#eq:spherical-continuation" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:spherical-continuation">[eq:spherical-continuation]</a>, every fixed admissible complexity range, $0<\kappa<1/10$, and $\eta>0$, there is a finite constant $C$ such that $$\label{eq:packet-source}
 \mathcal A_m(T_md)^3
       \le C m^{10\kappa+\eta}G_{\delta,1}(d)^3$$ for all its single-step maps and all its finite complex input arrays. The source also permits $A_{\delta,1}(d)^3$ on the right. The constant may depend on $\kappa$, $\eta$, the complexity range, the phase, and the windows.*


*In particular, suppose the initial labels $\nu\in\delta\mathbb Z^2$ and $z\in D_{\mathrm f}^{-1}\delta\mathbb Z^2$ lie in fixed bounded sets. Define $$\begin{aligned}
 S d(\xi)
 &=(D_{\mathrm f}\delta)^{-2}
     \sum_{\nu,z}d_{\nu,z}\chi_\delta^\nu(\xi)
                               e(-N^2z\cdot\xi),
                     \label{eq:packet-synthesis}\\
 c_{\nu',z',j}
 &=\int Sd(\xi)\chi_1^{\nu'}(\xi)
                 e\bigl(N^2z'\cdot\xi+j\phi(\xi)\bigr)\,d\xi,
                     \label{eq:packet-samples}
\end{aligned}$$ where $\nu'\in\mathbb Z^2$, $z'\in D_{\mathrm f}^{-1}N^{-2}\mathbb Z^2$, and $j=0,\ldots,N^2-1$. Retain any output coordinate mask for which $\nu',z'$ lie in fixed bounded sets. There is a finite $P(\kappa,\eta)\ge1$, depending on these fixed sets, the phase, and the windows, such that, for every dyadic $N\ge2$ and $0<\eta\le1$, $$\label{eq:packet-bound}
 N^{-6}\sum_{\nu',z',j}|c_{\nu',z',j}|^3
 \le P(\kappa,\eta)N^{10\kappa+\eta}
                  m(d)\mathcal K_\kappa(d)^{1/2}.$$ The sum is over the retained coordinates.*

There are fixed constants $C$ and $N_0$ such that for every dyadic $N\ge N_0$, every initial array $d$, and every $0<s\le1$, $$\label{eq:weighted-tubes}
 \int H_d^{3/2}
 \le C M(s)^{3/2}\delta^{-3s/2}
                 \delta^2\sum_\nu b_\nu(d)^{3/2}.$$ The constants $C,N_0$ depend only on the fixed geometric setup.

Let $d$ be a nonzero finite initial array, and let $n=\#\operatorname{supp}d$. For every $0<\kappa<1/10$, there is a decomposition into coordinate subarrays with pairwise disjoint supports, $$d=\sum_{i=1}^{J}d^{(i)},\qquad J\le C\log(2+n),$$ such that $$\label{eq:density-cost}
 \sum_{i=1}^{J}m(d^{(i)})\mathcal K_\kappa(d^{(i)})^{1/2}
 \le C\int H_d^{3/2}.$$ Both constants are absolute and independent of the nonzero coefficient sizes and of $\kappa$.

Let $c$ be the terminal coefficient array obtained from $d$ by the synthesis and sampling map of Theorem <a href="#thm:packet" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:packet">6</a>, with any fixed permitted output mask. For every sufficiently large dyadic $N$, $0<\kappa<1/10$, $0<\eta\le1$, and $0<s\le1$, put $\gamma_0=10\kappa+\eta+3s/2$. Then $$\label{eq:discrete-critical}
 \sum_{\nu',z',j}|c_{\nu',z',j}|^3
 \le C N^6 P(\kappa,\eta)M(s)^{3/2}
             N^{\gamma_0}\log^2(2+N)
             \delta^2\sum_\nu b_\nu(d)^{3/2}.$$ The constant $C$ and the lower threshold for $N$ are independent of $\kappa,\eta,s$. Their dependence is only on the fixed phase, windows, and label bounds; all additional parameter dependence is contained in $P(\kappa,\eta)$ and $M(s)$.

There is a constant $\mu_0\in(0,1)$ depending only on the fixed graph setup with the following property. Suppose that $h$ is supported in $Q_0$, $\|h\|_3\leq1$, and $0<\mu\leq\mu_0$. Let $\mathcal B$ be a finite family of balls of common radius $R$ such that $$\#\mathcal B\leq\mu^{-8},\qquad R\geq\mu^{-100},\qquad
 |x_B-x_{\widetilde B}|\geq\mu^{-40}R
 \quad(B\ne\widetilde B),$$ where $x_B$ denotes the center of $B$. Choose a dyadic integer $N$ with $4R\leq N^2<16R$. For $0<\kappa<1/10$ and $0<\eta,s\leq1$, put $\gamma_0=10\kappa+\eta+3s/2$. Then $$\label{eq:sparse-estimate}
 \left|\{|F|>\mu\}\cap\bigcup_{B\in\mathcal B}B\right|
 \leq C\mu^{-3}P(\kappa,\eta)M(s)^{3/2}
       N^{\gamma_0}\log^2(2+N).$$ The constant $C$ and the threshold $\mu_0$ are independent of $\kappa,\eta,s$, of the centers, and of their maximum separation.

Let $g\in L^2(\mathbb R^2)$ have compact support, and let $$G(y',t)=\int_{\mathbb R^2}g(\xi)
                  e\big(y'\cdot\xi+t\phi(\xi)\big)\,d\xi.$$ If $\phi''\geq cI$ on $\mathbb R^2$, then $$\label{eq:elementary-lfour}
 \|G\|_{L^4(\mathbb R^3)}
       \leq (\pi/c)^{1/4}\|g\|_{L^2(\mathbb R^2)}.$$ In particular, for $F=E_\phi(h\chi_1^{\nu_*})$ with $h$ supported in $Q_0$, there is a fixed $C_{\mathrm b}\geq1$ such that $$\label{eq:basic-bounds}
 \|F\|_4+\|F\|_\infty+\|\nabla F\|_\infty
       \leq C_{\mathrm b}\|h\|_3.$$

Let $0<\tau\leq1$, $0<\mu\leq\mu_0$, and let $X$ be the set in Lemma <a href="#lem:occupancy" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:occupancy">12</a>. Set $$\label{eq:cover-scales}
 \beta=\frac{\tau}{4},\qquad
 J=\left\lceil\frac{32}{\tau}\right\rceil+1,\qquad
 R_j=\mu^{-100(j+1)}\quad(0\leq j\leq J).$$ The union of the occupied cubes is covered by at most $$\label{eq:family-count}
 3J\bigl(1+8\log_2(1/\mu)\bigr)\mu^{-\beta}$$ families of balls. Each family has at most $\mu^{-8}$ balls, all of radius $R=4R_j$ for some $0\leq j<J$, and its centers are separated by at least $\mu^{-40}R$. For the dyadic $N$ chosen in Proposition <a href="#prop:sparse" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:sparse">10</a>, $$\label{eq:cover-N}
 4R\leq N^2<16R,\qquad N<8\mu^{-50J}.$$

Fix a phase and window setup for which Theorem <a href="#thm:packet" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:packet">6</a> and Proposition <a href="#prop:sparse" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:sparse">10</a> apply. For $3<p\leq4$, put $$\label{eq:theta-choice}
 \tau=p-3,\qquad \theta=10^{-6}\tau^2.$$ There is a constant $C$, independent of $p$ in this interval, such that $$\label{eq:global-graph-bound}
 \|E_\phi(h\chi_1^{\nu_*})\|_p
 \leq C\left[
       P(\theta,\theta)M(\theta)^{3/2}\tau^{-7}
       \right]^{1/p}\|h\|_3$$ for all $h\in L^3$ supported in $Q_0$. For $4<p<\infty$, Equation <a href="#eq:basic-bounds" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:basic-bounds">[eq:basic-bounds]</a> alone gives $\|E_\phi(h\chi_1^{\nu_*})\|_p\leq C_{\mathrm b}\|h\|_3$.

For every nonempty compact smooth surface $\Sigma$ in the stated class there is $c_\Sigma>0$ such that its best extension constant satisfies $$\label{eq:constant-lower-bound}
 C_{\Sigma,p}^{\mathrm{best}}
       \geq c_\Sigma(p-3)^{-1/p},\qquad 3<p\leq4.$$ This conclusion does not use the Kakeya or packet estimates.

Let $$\phi(x,t;y)=x\cdot y+\psi(t;y),\qquad
 x,y\in\mathbb R^2,\quad t\in\mathbb R,\quad \psi(0;y)=0,$$ be a fixed real smooth phase near the origin. Suppose throughout this neighborhood that $$M(t,y)=D_y^2\partial_t\psi(t;y)\quad\hbox{is definite},
 \qquad \partial_tM(t,y)=c(t,y)M(t,y)$$ for a smooth real scalar function $c$. On a sufficiently small fixed product chart, let $a$ be any fixed smooth amplitude with compact support in its interior. For every $3<p<\infty$, $$\label{eq:bourgain-oscillatory}
 \left\|\int e^{iN\phi(x,t;y)}a(x,t;y)f(y)\,dy
       \right\|_{L^p(\mathbb R^3_{x,t})}
 \le C_{\phi,a,p}N^{-3/p}\|f\|_{L^p(\mathbb R^2)},\qquad N\ge1,$$ where the operator is zero outside the output support of $a$. The constant may depend on the fixed chart, phase, amplitude and $p$, but not on $N$ or $f$.
